{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hqbk3Z7fJAShvwqjus8vWH","type":"tool_result","content":"    88→  // Update entry ref attributes when config changes\n    89→  useEffect(() => {\n    90→    const input = inputElRef.current\n    91→    if (!input) return\n    92→\n    93→    const joinEntries = (entries: UploadEntry[]) => entries.map(e => e.ref).join(\",\")\n    94→\n    95→    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-active-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries))\n    96→    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-done-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries.filter(e => e.done)))\n    97→    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-preflighted-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries.filter(e => e.preflighted)))\n    98→  }, [uploadConfig.entries])\n    99→\n   100→  // Derived state\n   101→  const entries = uploadConfig.entries || []\n   102→\n   103→  const progress = useMemo(() => {\n   104→    if (entries.length === 0) return 0\n   105→    const totalProgress = entries.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + (entry.progress || 0), 0)\n   106→    return Math.round(totalProgress / entries.length)\n   107→  }, [entries])\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e05a5f27-3808-43b9-9b1f-a81d138cf285","uuid":"8dd662b1-6953-4d0e-850c-977b4d55a966","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/assets/useLiveUploadReact.ts","content":"  // Update entry ref attributes when config changes\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const input = inputElRef.current\n    if (!input) return\n\n    const joinEntries = (entries: UploadEntry[]) => entries.map(e => e.ref).join(\",\")\n\n    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-active-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries))\n    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-done-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries.filter(e => e.done)))\n    input.setAttribute(\"data-phx-preflighted-refs\", joinEntries(uploadConfig.entries.filter(e => e.preflighted)))\n  }, [uploadConfig.entries])\n\n  // Derived state\n  const entries = uploadConfig.entries || []\n\n  const progress = useMemo(() => {\n    if (entries.length === 0) return 0\n    const totalProgress = entries.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + (entry.progress || 0), 0)\n    return Math.round(totalProgress / entries.length)\n  }, [entries])","numLines":20,"startLine":88,"totalLines":173}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019AMfqsakjwb7S22rTHi1nP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I see the issue now. The `useEffect` dependency is `[uploadConfig.entries]` - but since `uploadConfig` is a plain object prop that comes from LiveView, when LiveView updates the entries, React gets a whole new `uploadConfig` object. The `entries` array reference will be different each render, so the `useEffect` should fire. But wait - the debug output shows that `data-phx-active-refs` is empty and `Selected files: 1`. So the React component sees `entries.length` as 1 (from `entries = uploadConfig.entries || []`), but the `useEffect` isn't updating the DOM attribute.\n\nActually, looking more closely: the component shows \"Selected files: 1\" which means `entries.length` is 1. But `data-phx-active-refs` is empty. This means the `useEffect` with `[uploadConfig.entries]` dependency fires but gets the wrong/stale data, OR it hasn't fired yet at the time of the Playwright evaluation.\n\nWait - `useEffect` runs asynchronously after render. The `syncLV` function completes, and then we evaluate the DOM. But `useEffect` might not have run yet because React batches effects. Actually in React 18, `useEffect` should run after the DOM commit, and Playwright's `evaluate` waits for a microtask, so it should see the updated DOM...\n\nBut actually, the issue might be simpler. The `useEffect` at line 89 depends on `[uploadConfig.entries]`. React's comparison is by reference. If `uploadConfig.entries` is the same array reference between renders (because the parent passes the same prop object), the effect won't refire. But since LiveView sends new props, it should be a new array each time.\n\nLet me add more diagnostic output to understand the timing better."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":906,"cache_read_input_tokens":105616,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in